      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Ora protein powder is a well-made, unusually transparent organic plant protein: 23 g of protein per two-scoop serving, monk fruit as the only sweetener, Informed Sport certification and a published heavy-metal certificate for every lot, per Ora. What holds it back is cost, about $2.45 per 25 g on Amazon. This review covers the label, the heavy-metals question, price and who should buy it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Ora Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ora protein powder, sold as So Lean & So Clean, is one of the better organic plant proteins on the label and the most transparent on testing. Each two-scoop serving gives 23 g of protein from organic pea and rice protein, it is sweetened only with monk fruit, and Ora says every batch is Informed Sport certified and tested for lead, cadmium, arsenic and mercury, with the certificate posted online. The catch is price: about $2.45 per 25 g of protein at the Amazon price we saw, and about $3.26 at Ora's full $59.99 list price.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a vegan, USDA Organic protein with no stevia, no gums and a public heavy-metal record, and you can absorb the cost. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want 25 g or more of protein per scoop or the cheapest organic option, where <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain</a> costs roughly a fifth less.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Question</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Answer (per Ora's label and site)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g in 2 scoops (30-32 g)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic pea, rice, sacha inchi, amaranth, quinoa</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic monk fruit (none in unflavored)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Third-party testing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Informed Sport and Informed Protein, per Ora</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heavy metals</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lot certificates show pass against Ora's limits; exact amounts not published</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.45 on Amazon; about $3.26 at Ora's list price</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Ora So Lean & So Clean and the Closest Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ora sells So Lean & So Clean on Amazon through its own storefront, so the three main flavors are below with the prices we saw on November 20, 2026, followed by two organic plant proteins that cost less or carry a different certification.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Ora Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Ora says So Lean & So Clean is Informed Sport certified, which means samples are screened for substances banned in sport by LGC's lab under the Informed Sport program. Ora's product images state that every batch is tested for banned substances under Informed Sport, and its current supplement-facts panels carry both the Informed Sport logo and the Informed Protein seal, a separate LGC program that checks the protein content matches the label. Ora adds a note that older tins may not show the logo yet but are still certified.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not pull the product up in Informed Sport's own online search when we checked; the search page returned no results for our query, which may be a quirk of how the site loads. If you are a tested athlete, look up your tub's batch number at sport.wetestyoutrust.com before relying on the certification. That advice applies to every brand, not just Ora: the certification attaches to specific batches, not to a logo.
          </p>

          <p>
            On top of Informed Sport, Ora runs its own program it calls the 5 Nation Standard. According to <a href="https://ora.organic/pages/third-party-testing" rel="nofollow">Ora's testing page</a>, every production lot is sent to ISO 17025-accredited labs before release and tested for four heavy metals, six microbial markers and protein potency, while pesticides (about 70), residual solvents (28), allergens (8) and microplastics are checked on an annual schedule. Ora names Eurofins, FSNS, QALITEX and Certified Labs among the labs it uses.
          </p>

          <p>
            What separates Ora from most brands is that it publishes the result for each lot. Its certificates page lists So Lean & So Clean lots by flavor, with downloadable PDFs running about ten pages: heavy metals, microbiology, a solvent panel, a long pesticide screen and nutrition checks. Few organic plant brands do this. <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life</a> has NSF Certified for Sport on its Sport line, and <a href="/truvani-protein-powder-review/">Truvani</a> talks about testing, but neither publishes lot-by-lot results the same way.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE CAVEAT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Informed Sport screens for banned drugs, not lead</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Informed Sport and NSF Certified for Sport are anti-doping programs. They do not certify a product as low in heavy metals. For lead, the relevant evidence is Ora's own lot certificates, covered in the next section.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="heavy-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does Ora Protein Powder Have Heavy Metals?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ora's own lab certificates show So Lean & So Clean passing its heavy-metal limits, but they do not publish the measured amounts, and no independent test of Ora has been published. That is the honest answer to the 'ora protein powder heavy metals' question. The October 2026 certificate for chocolate lot S26204001 lists lead at not more than 5 micrograms per daily serving, cadmium at not more than 5, inorganic arsenic at not more than 15 and total mercury at not more than 15, each marked 'Meets Specification' and tested by ICP-MS. The vanilla and unflavored certificates we downloaded show the same limits and the same pass result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ora is unusually candid about why it does not print a 'free of heavy metals' claim. Its certificates page says trace amounts of naturally occurring elements exist in virtually all plant-based materials and that claiming otherwise would be dishonest. That matches the published research. Pea and rice protein pick up lead and cadmium from soil, and plant proteins test higher than dairy proteins as a group.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Ora's lead limit compares with other standards</h3>

          <p>
            Ora's 5 microgram limit for lead is half the 10 micrograms a day that its testing page cites as the USP adult limit for dietary supplements. It is ten times higher than the 0.5 micrograms a day that California's Proposition 65 uses as the level above which a warning is required, which is also the 'level of concern' Consumer Reports used in its October 2025 protein tests. So a lot can pass Ora's specification and still sit above the Prop 65 line; because the certificates show pass or fail rather than a number, you cannot tell where a given lot falls.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
