          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Do Buyers Say About Ora's Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly rate Ora's taste well for a plant protein: Ora's product page showed an average of 4.7 out of 5 from 4,845 reviews when we checked. We have not tasted it ourselves, and a brand's own review widget leans positive, so read that score as a sign that most buyers are satisfied rather than as proof.
          </p>

          <p>
            The praise that comes up most is that it is not too sweet and does not have the bitter aftertaste stevia leaves in many plant proteins. Ora's own FAQ makes the same point, and it markets the formula as created by its in-house chef after more than 100 test blends. Chocolate is the flavor buyers mention most often, with vanilla chai as the favorite among people who want something different. Published reviews of the powder describe it mixing without clumps in plain water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complaints follow from the formula. Because there are no gums, the shake is thinner than a gum-thickened powder such as Orgain, and buyers who want a milkshake texture tend to blend it with milk or a banana. Because the sweetness is mild, some buyers coming from whey find it plain in water. The unflavored version tastes of pea and greens, as unsweetened plant proteins do. And the most consistent complaint is price, made worse by the two-scoop serving, which empties a 20-serving tub in under three weeks of daily use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our practical advice: buy chocolate or vanilla chai if you drink shakes with water, vanilla if you blend smoothies, and unflavored only if you plan to cook with it or sweeten it yourself. Ora's FAQ suggests a date, banana or maple syrup for the unflavored tub. For more options ranked on flavor reports, see the <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
