        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Ora Protein Safe for Pregnancy, Kids and Food Allergies?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ora says none of its So Lean & So Clean ingredients are contraindicated in pregnancy or breastfeeding, but it recommends asking your doctor first, and that is the right call. A plant protein is a food, but heavy-metal exposure matters more during pregnancy and early childhood, and Ora's certificates do not give the measured lead content per lot. If you are pregnant, breastfeeding or buying for a child, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian, and consider asking Ora for the actual numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            For children, Ora's FAQ says many customers share the powder with their kids but tells parents to check with a doctor first. Most children get enough protein from food, and a 23 g serving is a large dose for a small child, so a powder is rarely necessary unless a clinician has suggested it.
          </p>

          <p>
            On allergens, Ora markets the powder as free of soy, dairy and gluten, and its certificate tests gluten at under 20 ppm. Two label details are worth knowing. The second ingredient is organic coconut milk, so anyone with a coconut allergy should avoid it. And the enzyme blend includes lactase, an enzyme that breaks down milk sugar; it does not mean the powder contains dairy, and the product is vegan, but it confuses some shoppers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone with kidney disease should check with their doctor before adding protein powder of any kind, and if you are on a sodium-restricted diet, count Ora's 230 mg or so per serving, which is less than many plant powders but not trivial. People on blood thinners or other medication should run the turmeric and superfood blend past a pharmacist, even though the amounts are likely small.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Ora Protein, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Ora if you want an organic vegan protein with no stevia, no gums and the most public testing record in its category, and you are comfortable paying about $2.45 per 25 g for it. It suits people who get bloated on gum-thickened powders, people who dislike the aftertaste of stevia, and athletes who need a plant protein with an Informed Sport claim behind it, provided they verify the batch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Ora if price is your main filter: Orgain and Garden of Life Sport both cost around a quarter less per gram on Amazon, and a whey protein costs far less again. Skip it if you want 25 to 30 g of protein from a single scoop, or if you have a coconut allergy.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if your main concern is heavy metals rather than organic certification, be clear about what Ora does and does not prove. It tests every lot and shows you the pass result, which is more than most brands do. It does not publish how much lead each lot contains, and no independent lab has published results for it. A dairy protein will usually test lower on lead than any plant blend, organic or not; our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">clean protein powder</a> guide covers options on both sides.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most buyers who have already decided on organic and plant-based, Ora is a sound choice, and the vanilla chai and chocolate tubs are the ones to start with. Use the 60-day guarantee on your first order, and buy through Amazon or a bundle on Ora's site rather than single tubs at full price.
          </p>
        </div>

