        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Informed Sport as a heavy-metal test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Informed Sport screens batches for substances banned in sport. It says nothing about lead or cadmium. For heavy metals, the evidence on Ora is its own lot certificates, which show a pass against a 5 microgram lead limit. Read the two claims separately, and do not assume a sport badge means a powder is low in metals.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading 'Meets Specification' as zero lead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ora's certificates mark each metal as meeting its limit, not as undetected. Ora itself says trace metals are present in virtually all plant ingredients. A lot can pass Ora's 5 microgram ceiling and still be above California's 0.5 microgram Prop 65 level. If the exact number matters to you, ask Ora for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying full list price for single tubs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At Ora's $59.99 list price, the protein costs about $3.26 per 25 g. The same tub was $44.99 on Amazon when we checked, and Ora's site offers a 25% discount on bundles plus a subscription. Paying list price for one tub at a time adds about a third to what you spend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting one scoop as a serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every Ora label lists a serving as two scoops. One scoop gives about 11 to 12 g of protein, not 23 g. Buyers who use one scoop think the tub lasts longer and then wonder why their protein intake falls short. Measure two scoops, or weigh 30 to 32 g, to get the labeled dose.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it for the superfood blend</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ora lists 15 fruits, vegetables and grains, but they share a small part of each 29.4 g serving and the label gives no individual amounts. They will not replace a portion of vegetables. Judge Ora on its 23 g of protein, its sweetener, its testing and its cost per 25 g, not on the greens.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

