            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ora protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Ora says So Lean & So Clean is Informed Sport certified for banned substances and carries the Informed Protein seal for protein content. Ora also sends every production lot to ISO 17025-accredited labs for heavy metals, microbes and potency, and publishes each lot's certificate on its website. Athletes should still look up their batch number on the Informed Sport site before competing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Ora protein powder have heavy metals?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It contains trace heavy metals, as all plant proteins do, and Ora says so openly. Its published lot certificates show lead under 5 micrograms per serving and cadmium under 5, marked as meeting specification, but they do not give measured amounts. Ora was not part of Consumer Reports' 2025 test, and we found no independent heavy-metal results for it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ora protein healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  On the label, yes. It is USDA Organic and vegan, with 23 g of protein, 120 to 130 calories, no added sugar, no gums and monk fruit as the only sweetener. The weaker points are a two-scoop serving and a protein density of about 72%, lower than isolate-based powders. Clean labels do not mean zero heavy metals, which applies to every plant protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Ora protein contain stevia or dairy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No to both, per Ora. The flavored tubs use organic monk fruit extract, and the unflavored tub has no sweetener at all. There is no dairy; the lactase listed in the enzyme blend is an enzyme, not a milk ingredient. The powder does contain organic coconut milk, so people with a coconut allergy should avoid it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Ora protein flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyers most often praise chocolate, and vanilla chai is the favorite among people who want a spiced flavor. Vanilla is the safer choice for smoothies because it blends into fruit. Unflavored is best for cooking or for people avoiding all sweeteners, but buyers say it tastes earthy on its own. We have not tasted them; this reflects buyer feedback.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the common complaints about Ora protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Price is the most common complaint: about $2.45 per 25 g on Amazon and more at Ora's list price, with a two-scoop serving that empties the tub quickly. Some buyers find the shake thin because there are no gums, and some coming from whey find it less sweet than expected. Ora's site still averaged 4.7 out of 5 from 4,845 reviews when we checked.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ora or Orgain protein better?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Ora is better on ingredients and testing: 23 g of protein versus 21 g, no stevia or gums, Informed Sport per Ora, and published lot certificates. Orgain is better on price, about $1.90 per 25 g against Ora's $2.45 at the Amazon prices we saw, and it is easier to find in stores. Consumer Reports rated Orgain okay for occasional use in its 2025 lead tests.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

